      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Mitosis is the fundamental process cells use to divide and grow, and understanding it helps students learn how life continues. Prophase is the first stage where chromosomes condense and become visible, while the nuclear envelope breaks down to allow movement. In metaphase, these chromosomes align precisely at the cell's equator, ensuring each new cell gets the right amount of genetic material. During anaphase, sister chromatids separate and are pulled to opposite poles by spindle fibers, a crucial step for accurate division. Finally, telophase occurs as two new nuclei form around the separated chromosomes, marking the end of mitosis. This detailed breakdown ensures you understand every step so you can learn effectively and help others learn too.",
